# Line feeds in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# Git for Windows checks text out with CRLF by default, and three things
# here read files rather than merely compiling them. None of the three says
# "the line endings are wrong" when it happens; each says something else,
# and the something else is what makes this worth stating rather than
# leaving to whatever the reader's git was installed with.
#
#   The golden fixtures. `tests/support`'s `check` compares the cell grid
#   it drew against `tests/fixtures/*.txt`, and `one_spelling` -- the one
#   normalising it does -- only turns `\` into `/`. A fixture checked out
#   with CRLF fails every one of them, and prints the two blocks under
#   "--- fixture ---" and "--- rendered ---" where they look identical.
#
#   `tests/fixtures/fake-agent.sh`. A POSIX shell reading CRLF keeps the
#   `\r` on the end of what it read, so the `case` arms stop matching and
#   the JSON it prints carries a stray byte. What that looks like from
#   obelus's end is an agent speaking the protocol badly.
#
#   The tree itself. `OBELUS_TREE` points a handful of tests at this
#   repository, because the two readings of a fold have to agree on shapes
#   nobody writes into a test and counting a tree is only worth asserting
#   on a real one. Those count bytes and lines of these files.
#
# Everything a reader writes here is text, and `text=auto` is what says so:
# git decides per file and leaves the rest alone. A few are not text -- the
# font the window carries, the screenshots the site shows, and the two
# containers built from `contrib/desktop/obelus.svg` for the two systems
# that will not take an SVG -- and those are named rather than left to be
# guessed at. Detection reads the first few kilobytes and these all have a
# NUL in the first four bytes, so it would in fact get every one of them
# right; what it would not survive is the next file somebody adds whose
# header happens to read as text.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.icns binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
